# libcss: read -webkit-appearance as appearance; legacy widget values as auto
# Generated 2026-09-30
#
# Scorecard r18-full-183's single most-dropped CSS declaration was
# `-webkit-appearance: none` (13 of 20 sites -- every webkit-flavoured page
# styles its form controls with the prefixed name); the legacy widget names
# (`button`, `textfield`, ...) dropped a further 4 (github, jquery, rte,
# wpblog). libcss accepted neither: the prefixed name was an unknown
# property (the declaration dropped whole) and the legacy values were not
# in the parser's value set.
#
# - src/parse/properties/properties.c: css__property_id's alias table (the
#   mask-properties aliases) gains -webkit-appearance -> APPEARANCE, so the
#   prefixed name reaches the unprefixed parser; the table is renamed
#   mask_aliases -> alias_props accordingly.
# - src/parse/properties/p_appearance.c: the parser's value set gains the
#   webkit legacy names, all read as auto -- on webkit they mean "use the
#   platform widget", which is exactly what auto means here (the engine
#   paints one built-in widget), so accepting them implies no new
#   rendering. An unrecognised value still drops.
#
# Test: CSS-appearance.html, 6 getComputedStyle cases (default auto;
# -webkit-appearance: none -> none; legacy button/textfield -> auto;
# unprefixed none unchanged; unknown value drops to auto).
